% Canonical bibliography for the RCMLR 2026 workshop paper.
%
% This file is the single source of truth for the paper's references and is what the
% LaTeX build reads: analysis/conference_rcmlr_leadtime/references.bib is a symlink to
% it, build_tex.py copies that into latex/references.bib, and build_package.py copies
% that into the submission bundle.
%
% PROVENANCE. Every entry was verified field by field against an authoritative registry
% -- Crossref (api.crossref.org/works/<DOI>), PubMed E-utilities, DataCite
% (api.datacite.org), or the issuing repository's own record -- on 2026-08-29. Author
% order and spelling are verified through at least the third author, and in full for
% works with fewer than three. The `s2:' comment above each entry records the key the
% same work carries in the hand-collected Semantic Scholar list, kept verbatim at
% bibtex/ref_checked.semanticscholar.bib; where the two disagreed the registry won, and
% every such case is listed in provenance/REFERENCE_RECONCILIATION_2026-08-29.md.
%
% CITATION KEYS ARE STABLE. They are the keys the manuscript's \cite commands use and
% must not be renamed for style. The Semantic Scholar export uses different keys; that
% mapping is the `s2:' comment, not a rename.
%
% Author lists ending in "and others" reproduce the source journal list's "et al"
% exactly. They are not truncations introduced here, and expanding one entry without
% the rest would make the list less consistent, not more.
%
% The `number' (issue) field is deliberately omitted except where the source list
% carried it. Volume plus page range or article number identifies every entry here
% unambiguously, and adding the field to some entries only would be worse than either
% extreme.
%
% This file ships inside the Overleaf bundle and may reach the anonymised mirror, so
% nothing in it -- comments included -- may indicate which entries share an author with
% this submission.

